window.SPEAKING_TASKS = [{"id": 1, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Mira just moved to Winnipeg for a new job and is renting her first apartment on her own. She is worried about managing her money because rent, utilities, and groceries are more expensive than she expected. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hi Mira, congratulations again on the new job and the new apartment. I can tell you're a bit stressed about money right now, so let me share a few ideas that might help. First, I think you should sit down and make a simple monthly budget. Write down your income, then list your fixed costs like rent and utilities, and after that your groceries and other spending. When you can see everything on paper, it becomes much easier to notice where your money is actually going and where you can cut back a little. Second, I really think you should look into a shared or discount grocery app, or even start meal planning for the week. A lot of people save a surprising amount just by shopping with a list instead of buying whatever looks good at the store. Doing this for even one month will show you a real pattern. Also, try setting up automatic transfers into a small savings account right after payday, even if it's a small amount, so you build a habit early. I know moving to a new city is already a big adjustment, and money stress on top of that is hard. But if you follow a budget and plan your meals, I really believe you'll feel more in control within a few weeks. You've got this.", "tips": "Focus on two or three concrete, practical suggestions (budgeting, meal planning, automatic savings) rather than vague comfort like 'don't worry.' A common mistake is spending too much time restating the problem instead of giving actual advice, so move to your recommendation quickly."}, {"id": 2, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your coworker Daniel has been offered a promotion at work, but it means he would have to travel to other cities two weeks every month. He has two young children at home and is unsure whether to accept the offer. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hey Daniel, I heard about the promotion, that's really exciting news, but I understand why you're torn about it. Honestly, I think the first thing you should do is talk this through with your partner before you decide anything, since this change affects your whole family, not just you. Sit down together and really think about how the travel schedule would work with school pickups, activities, and everyday routines. Second, I'd suggest asking your manager if there's any flexibility, maybe the travel could start smaller, like one week a month at first, or perhaps some of the trips could be done virtually instead. Companies are often more open to compromise than we expect, especially for a valued employee like you. It's also worth asking other people at your company who travel regularly how they manage it with their own families, since they might have practical tips you haven't thought of. At the end of the day, this is a big opportunity for your career, and it's completely normal to want to take it. But your kids are only young once, so make sure whatever you decide, you and your family are comfortable with the trade-off. Talk it over, ask about flexibility, and then trust your decision. I think you'll make the right call either way.", "tips": "Balance the career opportunity with the family concern instead of only pushing one side; naming a compromise (like reduced travel) shows nuanced thinking. Avoid simply saying 'take it' or 'don't take it' with no reasoning, since the task wants clear reasons behind your advice."}, {"id": 3, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your neighbour Tom keeps parking his car in front of your driveway, and it has happened four times this month. It makes it hard for you to leave for work on time. Tom seems friendly, and you don't want to damage the relationship. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Okay, I understand this is frustrating, especially when it keeps making you late for work. Here's what I think you should do. First, don't let this build up into resentment, go talk to Tom directly and calmly, ideally in person rather than leaving an angry note on his windshield. Notes can come across as rude and might start unnecessary tension between neighbours. Just knock on his door one evening and mention it in a friendly way, something like, hey Tom, I noticed your car has blocked my driveway a few times this month, and it's made me late a couple of times, could you maybe park a bit further down the street? Most of the time, people genuinely don't realize they're causing a problem, so a simple, polite conversation usually solves it right away. Second, if it keeps happening after you've spoken with him, I'd suggest putting up a small, clear sign near your driveway as a gentle reminder for anyone parking nearby. And if it becomes a real pattern even after that, you could always check with your city about local parking rules, since blocking a driveway is often against bylaws. But honestly, I think a friendly conversation will fix this before it ever gets to that point. Just be direct, be kind, and give him the benefit of the doubt.", "tips": "Emphasize a calm, direct conversation as the first step since this keeps the relationship intact, which matches the scenario's concern. A common mistake is jumping straight to calling the city or being confrontational, which ignores the detail that you want to stay on good terms with Tom."}, {"id": 4, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your younger cousin Aisha is starting college in Halifax next month and wants to know how to make new friends quickly since she doesn't know anyone in the city. She is naturally quite shy and is nervous about the whole situation. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hi Aisha, I remember feeling exactly the same way before I started school somewhere new, so I really understand your nerves. Let me give you a few suggestions that I think will help a lot. First, I'd say join a club or activity in your first two weeks, even if it feels a little scary. Whether it's a sports team, a music group, or a volunteer club, these are the easiest places to meet people, because you already share something in common right from the start. You don't have to be the most outgoing person in the room, you just have to show up regularly, and friendships will naturally build over time. Second, try to be one of the first people to say hello in your classes. Something as small as asking to sit beside someone or asking about an assignment can open the door to a real conversation. People are usually just as nervous as you are, so a small effort from you goes a long way. Also, don't be afraid to invite a classmate for coffee between classes, most people are happy to be asked. I know being shy makes all of this feel harder, but you don't need to change who you are, you just need small, consistent steps. Join something, say hello first, and give it time. I promise you'll have a good group of friends before you know it.", "tips": "Give practical, low-pressure suggestions (joining a club, saying hello first) that respect the person's shyness rather than telling them to simply 'be more confident.' Avoid generic advice with no specifics, since concrete actions sound more genuine and score better than vague encouragement."}, {"id": 5, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your brother-in-law Kevin bought a used car two months ago, and it has already broken down twice. He is trying to decide whether to keep paying for repairs or trade it in for something more reliable. He asked for your opinion. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hey Kevin, that sounds really frustrating, especially since the car is still so new to you. Here's how I'd think about this. First, before deciding anything, I'd take the car to a trusted mechanic, not the dealership, and ask for an honest inspection of what's actually wrong and roughly what future repairs might cost. Sometimes a car has one or two unlucky issues that are fixable and cheap, and other times a mechanic can tell you it's likely to keep breaking down. That information will really guide your decision. Second, once you know the repair costs, compare that total to what you'd realistically get for a trade-in plus the cost of a different reliable used car. If the repairs are going to cost more than about a third of the car's value, or if the mechanic thinks more problems are likely, I honestly think trading it in makes more financial sense in the long run, even though it feels like a loss right now. On the other hand, if it's something small like a battery or a sensor, it's probably worth just fixing it and moving on. I know this is stressful, nobody wants car trouble this soon after buying something. But get that mechanic's opinion first, then compare the real numbers, and you'll be able to make a decision you feel confident about instead of just guessing.", "tips": "Ground the advice in a clear decision-making method (get an inspection, compare repair cost to value) rather than just saying 'keep it' or 'sell it.' A common mistake is giving advice with no reasoning attached, so always explain why each step matters."}, {"id": 6, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Priya's teenage son has started spending most of his free time on his phone and video games, and his grades have started to drop this semester. Priya doesn't want to cause a big fight at home but knows something needs to change. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hi Priya, I know this is a tough situation, especially because you don't want to turn it into a huge conflict at home. Here's what I would suggest. First, instead of taking the phone away suddenly, which usually just causes a fight and resentment, try sitting down with your son calmly and having an honest conversation about what you've noticed with his grades. Ask him how he feels about school right now, without sounding like you're accusing him of anything. Teenagers respond much better when they feel heard rather than punished. Second, once you've talked, try setting clear and reasonable screen time limits together, as a team, rather than just announcing rules. For example, maybe phones and games are put away during homework hours and after a certain time at night, but he still gets fair free time on weekends. When he has some say in the plan, he's much more likely to actually follow it. It also helps to check in with him weekly about how his classes are going, just casually, not like an interrogation. I know it's hard to balance being supportive and being firm, especially with teenagers, but a calm conversation and a shared plan usually works better than strict rules forced on him. Give it a try, and check back in with him regularly, I think you'll see real improvement.", "tips": "Show that you understand the emotional side of parenting a teenager, then offer a collaborative solution instead of a strict punishment, since the prompt specifically says the parent wants to avoid a fight. Avoid advice that ignores this detail, like simply saying to take the phone away completely."}, {"id": 7, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Sam recently started working from home full time and says he has been feeling isolated and unmotivated. He used to enjoy going into the office and seeing his coworkers every day. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hey Sam, I'm sorry to hear you've been feeling that way, working from home can definitely be a big adjustment, especially if you were used to being around people every day. I have a few ideas that might help. First, I think you should try setting up a regular routine, almost like you're still commuting. Get dressed, start work at the same time each day, and take real breaks away from your screen, maybe a short walk outside around lunch. Having that structure can make a huge difference for your motivation and your mood. Second, try to build some social contact back into your week on purpose, since it won't happen naturally anymore like it did at the office. You could suggest a weekly video call with your team just to chat, not only about work, or even meet a coworker or friend for coffee once a week. Some people also find it helpful to work a couple of days a week from a coffee shop or a coworking space, just to be around other people again. It doesn't have to be a big change, even small things like a daily walk and one planned social activity each week can really help with the isolation. I know working from home has a lot of benefits too, so it's really about finding the right balance. Try adding structure and connection back in, and I think you'll start feeling more like yourself.", "tips": "Address both the practical routine problem and the social isolation separately, since the prompt mentions two distinct issues. A common mistake here is only talking about productivity tips and forgetting to address the loneliness, which is really the emotional core of the problem."}, {"id": 8, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Julia's elderly father lives alone in Regina and recently had a small fall in his kitchen. He is still independent and doesn't want to move, but Julia is worried about his safety when she isn't there. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hi Julia, I can imagine how worried you must be after hearing about your dad's fall, but I understand why he wants to stay independent too, so let's think about a solution that respects both things. First, I'd suggest looking into a medical alert device, the kind he can wear as a bracelet or a necklace, so if he falls again, he can call for help right away even if you're not there. A lot of these services also do regular check-in calls, which could give both of you peace of mind. Second, it might be worth having someone come do a simple safety check of his home, looking at things like loose rugs, poor lighting, or a lack of grab bars near the bathtub. Small changes like adding a grab bar or better lighting in the kitchen can prevent a lot of falls without changing his lifestyle at all. You could also ask about a local home care visit, even just once or twice a week, so someone checks in on him without it feeling like he's losing his independence. I really think the key here is finding options that keep him safe without making him feel like he's being forced out of his home. Talk to him gently about these ideas, and I bet he'll be more open to them than you expect, especially if he understands they're about safety, not control.", "tips": "Respect the father's desire for independence while still giving safety-focused advice, since the prompt makes clear this balance matters. Avoid advice that jumps straight to 'he should move into a care home,' as it ignores the stated wish to stay independent."}, {"id": 9, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Marco has saved enough money to either take a long trip through Europe or use it as a down payment on a small condo in Vancouver. He keeps going back and forth and asked for your honest opinion. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hey Marco, that's a great problem to have, honestly, but I understand why it feels like such a big decision. Here's how I'd look at it. First, I think you should ask yourself which option you'll regret not doing more, five or ten years from now. Property in Vancouver will probably still be available in some form later, but trips like this, with the freedom and timing you have right now, aren't always easy to repeat once life gets busier with a career or a family. That said, buying a condo also builds long-term financial stability, so it's not a simple choice either way. Second, look at your actual financial picture. If putting all your savings into a down payment would leave you with very little safety net, that's risky, and maybe it makes more sense to travel now and save toward a home over the next couple of years instead. On the other hand, if the housing market in your area is moving fast and prices keep rising, waiting could cost you more later. I'd also suggest talking to a mortgage advisor, just to understand realistically what you could afford right now versus in two or three years. There's no wrong answer here, but I'd lean toward thinking about timing and regret first, then let the numbers guide the rest. Whatever you choose, I think you'll be excited either way.", "tips": "Present a genuine trade-off with reasoning on both sides before landing on a recommendation, since this scenario is a true dilemma rather than an obvious problem. A common mistake is giving a flat one-word answer like 'buy the condo' without walking through the reasoning, which sounds less convincing to a listener."}, {"id": 10, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your coworker Lisa has been asked to give a presentation to senior management next week, and she has never done public speaking before. She is extremely nervous and has asked you for advice on how to prepare. Give this person your advice.", "model_answer": "Hi Lisa, first of all, congratulations, being asked to present to senior management means they trust your work, so try to see this as a good opportunity, even though I totally understand the nerves. Here's what I'd suggest to help you prepare. First, don't just write out your slides and hope for the best, actually practice out loud, several times, ideally in front of a mirror or a friend, or even record yourself on your phone. Hearing yourself speak helps you notice where you stumble or where your explanation isn't clear yet, and fixing that ahead of time will make you feel much more confident on the actual day. Second, keep your slides simple, just key points and images, not full paragraphs of text, because reading directly off the screen usually makes presentations feel stiff and actually increases anxiety, since you're worried about losing your place. Instead, use the slides as a guide and speak naturally around them. It also really helps to prepare for two or three questions you think management might ask, so you're not caught off guard. And on the day itself, remember to just slow down and breathe, most people speak too fast when they're nervous. Practice out loud, simplify your slides, and prepare for questions, and I really think you're going to do better than you expect. You've got this, Lisa.", "tips": "Focus on concrete preparation steps (practicing aloud, simplifying slides, anticipating questions) rather than only saying 'don't be nervous,' since specific actionable advice sounds more credible. Avoid rushing through the answer, pace yourself so you naturally fill close to the full 90 seconds with real content."}, {"id": 11, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a memorable trip you took. Say where you went, who you went with, and why it was memorable for you.", "model_answer": "I want to talk about a trip I took to Vancouver Island two summers ago with my sister. We drove up from the ferry terminal and stayed in a small cabin near Tofino for five days. What made it memorable was the whale watching tour we booked on our second day. The water was calm and about twenty minutes into the boat ride, we saw a pod of orcas swimming right beside us. Our guide cut the engine so we could just watch quietly. My sister and I had never seen anything like that in person, and we both got a little emotional. Afterward, we walked along the beach at sunset and talked about how lucky we felt. That trip really brought us closer together, and it reminded me how important it is to slow down and appreciate nature. I still think about that moment on the water whenever I feel stressed.", "tips": "Anchor your answer with one clear moment (the whale sighting) rather than listing the whole itinerary. Use past tense consistently and finish with how the experience made you feel to give it a natural ending."}, {"id": 12, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you helped someone. Say what happened, who was involved, and how it turned out.", "model_answer": "Last winter, my elderly neighbour slipped on the ice in front of her house and hurt her ankle. I was walking home from work when I saw her sitting on the ground, so I ran over right away. She couldn't put weight on her foot, so I helped her up carefully and supported her while we walked slowly back inside. I called her daughter and stayed with her until she arrived about twenty minutes later. We also put some salt down on the icy spot so nobody else would fall. In the end, her ankle was just sprained, not broken, and she was really grateful. A few days later, she brought over a plate of cookies to thank me. That small moment taught me how much a quick response can matter, and now I always check on her when it snows, just to make sure she's doing okay.", "tips": "Give concrete actions in order (saw, ran, helped, called) rather than general statements like I helped her a lot. End by explaining the outcome and what you learned or how the relationship changed afterward."}, {"id": 13, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a challenge you overcame. Explain what the challenge was, what you did about it, and what the result was.", "model_answer": "A few years ago, I struggled a lot when I first moved to a new city for a job and didn't know anyone. For the first two months, I felt really isolated and almost regretted taking the position. I decided to push myself out of my comfort zone by joining a local recreational soccer league on weekends. At first it was awkward because I didn't know the rules well and felt shy talking to strangers. But after a few practices, I started chatting with teammates and eventually a couple of us started grabbing coffee after games. Within about six months, I had a solid group of friends and actually felt at home in the city. Looking back, that challenge taught me that putting myself in uncomfortable situations is usually worth it. Now whenever I move somewhere new, I remind myself to find one activity right away instead of waiting for things to happen naturally.", "tips": "Clearly separate the problem from the solution: name the challenge first, then describe the specific action you took to solve it. A short reflection at the end (what you learned) makes the story feel complete."}, {"id": 14, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you learned something new. Say what you learned, how you learned it, and why it mattered to you.", "model_answer": "Two years ago, I decided to learn how to bake bread from scratch after watching a few videos online during the winter. At first, it was harder than I expected. My first two loaves came out dense and barely rose at all. I did some research and realized I wasn't letting the dough rest long enough and my kitchen was too cold for the yeast to work properly. So I started proofing the dough near the oven and gave it more time. By my fourth attempt, I finally got a loaf with a nice crust and a soft inside. It felt like a real accomplishment, honestly. Now I bake bread almost every weekend and even share loaves with my neighbours. Learning that skill mattered to me because it showed me that patience and small adjustments can turn a failure into something you're genuinely proud of.", "tips": "Show the learning process, not just the final skill: mention what went wrong first and how you adjusted. Ending with how the skill is still part of your life now gives the story a satisfying close."}, {"id": 15, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a mistake you made and what you learned from it. Say what happened, how you dealt with it, and what the outcome was.", "model_answer": "A couple of years ago, I forgot to double check a flight booking and accidentally booked my ticket for the wrong day, a full week before my actual vacation started. I only realized my mistake when I got a reminder email from the airline the night before. I panicked for a moment, but then I called customer service right away to see what my options were. Luckily, they let me change the date for a fairly small fee since I caught it early. It cost me some money I hadn't planned to spend, but it could have been much worse. After that experience, I started setting calendar reminders and always reread my confirmation emails carefully before closing the booking window. It was a stressful evening, but honestly it made me a lot more organized with travel planning, and I haven't made a mistake like that since.", "tips": "Be honest and specific about the mistake itself rather than vague. Focus most of your time on how you fixed it and the habit you built afterward, since that shows growth clearly to the examiner."}, {"id": 16, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a moment you felt proud of yourself. Say what you accomplished, why it mattered, and how you felt afterward.", "model_answer": "Last spring, I completed my first half marathon after training for almost four months. I had never run more than five kilometres before, so signing up felt intimidating. I followed a training plan three times a week, even on days when I really didn't feel like getting out of bed early. On race day, the weather was cold and rainy, and around the sixteen kilometre mark, my legs started cramping badly. I honestly thought about stopping, but I kept a slow, steady pace and focused on just finishing. When I crossed the finish line, I actually teared up a little. My family was there cheering, which made it even more special. That moment mattered to me because it proved I could stick to a difficult goal even when it got uncomfortable. I still keep my finisher medal on my desk as a reminder of that day.", "tips": "Describe the effort leading up to the moment, not just the result, so the pride feels earned. A physical detail like crossing the finish line or a specific reaction makes the ending vivid and easy to picture."}, {"id": 17, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you had to adapt to a big change. Say what the change was, how you adjusted, and how things turned out.", "model_answer": "When my company switched to fully remote work a few years ago, it was a huge adjustment for me. I had always relied on being in the office to stay focused and connected with my team. For the first few weeks, I struggled with distractions at home and felt disconnected from my coworkers. To adapt, I set up a proper workspace in a spare room and created a strict daily schedule with set start and end times. I also started scheduling short video calls with teammates just to chat, not only for work tasks, to keep that sense of connection. It took about a month, but eventually I actually became more productive than I had been in the office. My work life balance improved too, since I saved so much commuting time. Looking back, that change pushed me to build habits that I still use today, even now that hybrid work is an option.", "tips": "Name the change clearly at the start so the listener immediately understands the situation. Walk through your adjustment step by step and end with a lasting positive result to show adaptability."}, {"id": 18, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a gift you gave or received that was meaningful to you. Say what the gift was, the occasion, and why it meant so much.", "model_answer": "For my dad's sixtieth birthday last year, my siblings and I put together a photo album filled with pictures from throughout his life, from his childhood in Halifax to more recent family gatherings. We spent weeks collecting old photos from relatives and writing little captions for each page. On the day of his party, we gave it to him after dinner, and he opened it slowly, looking at each page carefully. He got quite emotional, especially when he reached a photo of his own parents, who had passed away years earlier. The whole room went quiet for a moment. It meant so much because it wasn't something we bought, it was something we built together as a family, showing him how much his life had touched all of us. He still keeps that album on his coffee table, and he shows it to visitors all the time.", "tips": "Explain why the gift was meaningful, not just what it was, since that emotional detail is what examiners listen for. Describing the recipient's reaction in the moment makes the story feel real and specific."}, {"id": 19, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you worked as part of a team. Say what the task was, what your role was, and how the team worked together.", "model_answer": "A good example is when I volunteered to help organize a community food drive at my local church last fall. There were about eight of us on the planning team, and we each took on a different job. My role was contacting local grocery stores to ask for donations, while others handled advertising, sorting donations, and setting up the event space. In the beginning, communication was a bit messy because we weren't using a shared system to track progress. We solved that by setting up a group chat and having a quick check in every few days. In the end, we collected over two thousand pounds of food, way more than we expected, and the whole team celebrated together afterward. What I liked most was seeing how everyone's different strengths came together to make something successful that none of us could have done alone.", "tips": "Clearly state your specific role within the team, not just what the group did overall. Mention one small obstacle the team faced and solved together, since that shows real collaboration rather than a perfect story."}, {"id": 20, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you tried something for the first time. Say what it was, how you felt, and what happened.", "model_answer": "Last summer, I tried kayaking for the first time on a lake near Muskoka with a couple of friends who had done it before. Honestly, I was pretty nervous getting into the kayak because I'm not the strongest swimmer, and the instructor's safety talk made me even more anxious. Once we pushed off from the shore, though, it felt surprisingly natural after the first few minutes. My friends paddled beside me and gave me tips on how to steer properly, since I kept drifting sideways at first. About halfway across the lake, I actually relaxed and started enjoying the quiet and the view of the mountains around us. We ended up staying out on the water for almost two hours. By the end, I felt proud that I had pushed through my nerves, and I've already booked another kayaking trip for this coming summer.", "tips": "Acknowledge your nervousness honestly at the start, since that contrast with how you felt by the end makes the story stronger. Keep the middle section focused on one or two specific moments rather than summarizing the whole activity."}, {"id": 21, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "Okay, this picture shows a park on a sunny afternoon, and it looks like a really nice day out. In the background, I can see three cyclists riding along a path at the far edge of the park, probably just getting some exercise. Closer to the middle, there's a large oak tree, and under it, two children are flying a red kite. It looks like they're having a lot of fun trying to keep it up in the air. Nearby, an elderly man is sitting on a wooden bench, reading a newspaper, just relaxing and enjoying the quiet. In the foreground, there's a small fountain, and a dog is chasing a tennis ball right beside it, probably playing fetch with its owner. Also in the foreground, a woman is jogging along a paved path, maybe doing her daily workout. Overall, it's a lively, relaxed scene showing people of all ages enjoying the park in different ways.", "tips": "Move through the scene in layers, from the cyclists in the background to the fountain and jogger in the foreground, so your description feels organized rather than random. Practise the phrase 'it looks like' to naturally guess at people's activities and intentions without slowing down.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_21_a.jpg"}, {"id": 22, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows an outdoor farmers market on what looks like a Saturday morning, and there's a lot going on. Near a lamppost, a busker is playing an acoustic guitar, probably entertaining the shoppers as they walk by. Close to him, a vendor is arranging baskets of red apples on a wooden table, getting the stall ready for customers. Right beside that stall, a woman is weighing a bag of tomatoes on a small scale, so she's probably deciding how much to buy. A bit further along, a young boy is tasting a sample of cheese that another vendor is offering him, and he looks curious about trying something new. In the foreground, two friends are chatting with each other while carrying reusable shopping bags, so they're likely doing their weekly grocery shopping together. Overall, this looks like a friendly, relaxed community market where people are shopping, socializing, and enjoying some live music at the same time.", "tips": "Farmers market scenes have lots of small actions happening at once, so pick one clear verb for each person or vendor and stick to it rather than over-describing. Mention the reusable bags to add a natural Canadian touch that examiners recognize.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_22_a.jpg"}, {"id": 23, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows an elementary school classroom during what looks like a science lesson. At the front of the room, the teacher is standing beside the whiteboard, pointing at a diagram of the solar system, so she seems to be explaining the planets to the class. In the front row, two students are raising their hands, probably trying to answer a question the teacher just asked. Near the window, there's a boy who's looking through a small telescope model sitting on his desk, so he looks really interested in the topic. At the back of the classroom, a girl is writing notes in her notebook, maybe copying down what the teacher is saying. Over by the door, there's a shelf with a stack of textbooks on it, which the students probably use for other subjects. Overall, this looks like an engaging science class where the students seem curious and involved in learning about space.", "tips": "For classroom scenes, sweep from the teacher and whiteboard at the front to the students in the back, since that mirrors how a real classroom is organized. Use words like 'seems' and 'probably' when guessing what the teacher is explaining, since you can't know the exact lesson content.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_23_a.jpg"}, {"id": 24, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a home kitchen in the early evening, and it looks like a family is preparing dinner together. Near the stove, a man is chopping carrots on a cutting board, probably getting ingredients ready for the meal. Right behind him, there's a pot of soup simmering on the back burner, and I can see steam rising from it. On the counter, a young girl is standing on a step stool, stirring a bowl of batter, so she's likely helping to make dessert or maybe some muffins. Over by the window, a cat is sitting on the windowsill, just watching all the activity happening in the kitchen. Near the sink, there's a loaf of bread cooling on a wire rack, which looks like it was baked earlier. Overall, this looks like a warm, cozy family scene where everyone is working together to prepare a home-cooked meal.", "tips": "Kitchen scenes work well when you group actions by task, cooking the main meal versus baking, so your description flows logically. Include sensory details like the steam rising from the pot to make your answer sound more vivid and natural.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_24_a.jpg"}, {"id": 25, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a city bus stop in the early morning, and it looks like light snow is falling, so it's probably a cold winter day. In the background, a city bus is pulling up to the curb, and its doors are just starting to open. Near the shelter, a teenager is leaning against the wall, listening to music through headphones, and seems to be waiting patiently. Close to the curb, a man is helping an elderly woman carry her grocery bags toward the bus doors, which looks really kind of him. Beside them, a woman in a winter coat is checking the time on her phone, maybe making sure she's not going to be late for work. On the sidewalk, you can see footprints in the fresh snow from people walking by earlier. Overall, this looks like a typical Canadian winter morning at a busy bus stop, with people helping each other and getting ready to start their day.", "tips": "Winter transit scenes are a great chance to show Canadian context naturally, so mention the snow and cold weather early in your answer. Highlight the man helping the elderly woman, since describing a kind action adds detail and keeps your response interesting for the full 60 seconds.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_25_a.jpg"}, {"id": 26, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a sandy lakeside beach on a warm summer day, and there's a lot of activity happening. Out on the water, a couple is paddling a canoe a short distance from the shore, enjoying the sunny weather. Closer to the beach, a lifeguard is sitting on an elevated wooden chair, watching the swimmers to make sure everyone stays safe. Nearby, two teenagers are playing beach volleyball over a low net, and they look really focused on the game. In the middle of the beach, a young boy is building a sandcastle with a red plastic bucket, and he seems really proud of what he's making. In the foreground, a family is setting up a striped umbrella and some folding chairs near the water, probably getting ready to spend the whole afternoon there. Overall, this looks like a relaxing summer day at the beach, with people of different ages enjoying various activities by the lake.", "tips": "Use the lifeguard and canoe in the water as your starting point since they naturally anchor the background before you move toward the busier foreground. Vary your verbs, paddling, playing, building, setting up, so your description doesn't repeat the same action words.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_26_a.jpg"}, {"id": 27, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a downtown construction site on what looks like a weekday morning, with a lot of work happening. Up above, a crane is lifting a steel beam toward the top of a partially built structure, so the building looks like it's still under construction. Below that, a supervisor is speaking into a walkie-talkie while pointing toward the crane, probably giving instructions to the crew. Nearby, two workers wearing orange vests and hard hats are standing at a folding table, reviewing blueprints together. A bit further over, a man is operating a small excavator near a pile of gravel, and it looks like he's moving material around the site. Around the whole area, there's yellow warning tape marking the perimeter, which keeps people at a safe distance. Overall, this looks like a busy, well-organized construction site where everyone has a specific job to do and safety seems to be a real priority.", "tips": "Start high with the crane and beam, then move down to ground-level workers, since construction sites naturally have a vertical structure worth describing top to bottom. Mention the safety gear and warning tape, as noticing these details shows strong observation skills to the examiner.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_27_a.jpg"}, {"id": 28, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a living room decorated for a child's birthday party, and everyone seems to be having a great time. Near the window, there's a side table with wrapped gifts stacked on it, probably waiting to be opened later. Around the main table, colourful balloons are tied to the backs of the chairs, which really adds to the festive mood. Close to that table, a woman is placing candles on a chocolate cake shaped like the number seven, so it looks like the child is turning seven years old. On the floor, three children are wearing paper party hats and playing a board game together, and they all look really happy and excited. In the foreground, a man is taking a photo of the group with his phone, probably capturing the moment to remember later. Overall, this looks like a fun, cheerful birthday celebration with family and friends gathered together to celebrate a special day.", "tips": "Use the cake's number seven candles to naturally explain the occasion instead of just saying 'a birthday party,' since specific details make your description sound more observant. Keep your pace steady since party scenes have several small groups of people, and rushing through them can make your answer sound like a list.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_28_a.jpg"}, {"id": 29, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a grocery store aisle on what looks like a weekday evening, with several people shopping and working at the same time. Near the end of the aisle, a store employee is mopping up a small spill on the floor, probably making sure it's safe for customers to walk through. In the middle of the aisle, a stock clerk is stacking cans of soup onto a shelf from a cart, restocking the store for the next day. Close by, a woman is comparing prices on two boxes of cereal, so she looks like she's trying to find the better deal. Nearby, an older man is reading the label on a jar of pasta sauce, maybe checking the ingredients before buying it. In the foreground, a young boy is sitting in the front seat of a shopping cart, holding a box of crackers, and he looks pretty happy about it. Overall, this looks like a typical, busy evening at the grocery store, with customers shopping and staff keeping things running smoothly.", "tips": "Grocery scenes are easy to over-list, so anchor each person to a clear reason for their action, like comparing prices or checking a label, to keep your description natural. Save the shopping cart with the boy for last, since ending on a warm, relatable detail leaves a strong final impression.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_29_a.jpg"}, {"id": 30, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. In this scene, describe everything you see happening.", "model_answer": "This picture shows an open-concept office space during the workday, and there's a mix of both focused work and casual conversation happening. Near the entrance, there's a printer with a stack of file folders sitting on top of it, probably waiting to be picked up. Close to the window, a man is typing quickly at a standing desk while wearing headphones, so he looks really focused on finishing a task. Beside him, a young employee is watering a plant on the windowsill, taking a short break from work to look after it. In the corner, two coworkers are chatting near the coffee machine, maybe catching up before getting back to their desks. In the middle of the office, a woman is presenting a chart on a large screen to three colleagues seated around a meeting table, so it looks like an important team meeting is taking place. Overall, this looks like a modern, busy workplace where employees are balancing individual tasks with teamwork and collaboration.", "tips": "Office scenes combine a formal meeting with casual moments, so contrast the presentation at the table with the relaxed coffee chat to show range in your description. Practise transition phrases like 'in the corner' and 'in the middle of the office' to move smoothly between different groups of people.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_30_a.jpg"}, {"id": 31, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the boy is going to blow out the candles in the next few seconds, and everyone around him is going to clap and sing happy birthday again. After that, the parents will probably start cutting the cake and handing out slices while the kids open their gift bags. But because those dark storm clouds are rolling in, I think the party might have to move indoors pretty soon. Someone will likely notice the sky first and suggest bringing the food and gifts inside before it starts raining. If they are lucky, they might get a few more minutes to take photos first. Eventually, I think everyone is going to end up finishing the celebration inside the house, and the outdoor decorations could get wet if nobody grabs them in time. Overall, this party is going to be cut a bit short by the weather.", "tips": "Anchor your prediction to both visible clues, the candles and the storm clouds, and connect them with a time sequence. Use a mix of going to and might to show different levels of certainty.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_31_a.jpg"}, {"id": 32, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the man is going to keep walking straight toward that icy patch because he is looking at his phone instead of watching where he is going. In the next few seconds, his foot is going to hit the ice and he might slip. He could lose his balance and either fall down or manage to catch himself at the last second. If he falls, his coffee cup will probably go flying and spill, and his briefcase might drop too. The woman across the street looks worried, so she is probably going to call out a warning, but it might come too late. After that, a few people nearby will likely notice and rush over to check if he is okay. Eventually, he will probably get up, feel embarrassed, brush off his coat, and keep walking, but much more carefully this time.", "tips": "Focus on the cause and effect between the phone distraction and the icy patch. Describe the fall as a possibility using might or could, since we cannot be one hundred percent certain he will actually slip.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_32_a.jpg"}, {"id": 33, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think that pot of water is going to boil over in the next few seconds because the foam is already rising close to the edge. Since the woman has her back turned and is chopping vegetables, she probably will not notice right away. The water is going to spill over the sides and hit the hot stovetop, and it might make a sizzling sound or a small mess on the burner. The timer is about to ring, so that will likely be what finally gets her attention. When she hears the beep, she is going to turn around quickly and rush to the stove to lower the heat or move the pot. After that, she will probably grab a cloth to wipe up the spilled water and check the pasta. In the end, I do not think it will be a big disaster, just a small kitchen mishap she will clean up quickly.", "tips": "Use the timer as a natural trigger point in your story, since it explains why she will suddenly react. Keep your prediction realistic and small in scale, since a boil over is a common everyday event, not a dramatic one.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_33_a.jpg"}, {"id": 34, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the woman is not going to catch this bus in time. The doors are already closed and the bus is starting to move, so even though she is running and waving her arm, I do not think the driver is going to notice her. In the next few seconds, the bus is probably going to pull away and merge into traffic, leaving her behind. She might slow down once she realizes it is hopeless, and her bag could slip right off her shoulder as she runs. After she stops running, she is going to catch her breath and check the schedule sign to see when the next bus arrives. Since it says twenty minutes, she will probably feel frustrated and might message someone that she will be late. Eventually, I think she will just find a spot to wait for the next bus.", "tips": "Be clear and confident that she will miss the bus, since the doors are already closed, then shift to describing her reaction afterward. Use the schedule sign detail to predict her next practical step, checking the time and waiting.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_34_a.jpg"}, {"id": 35, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think this picnic is going to be interrupted soon because of the weather. Those dark clouds moving in and the wind blowing napkins off the blanket are strong signs rain is coming. In the next few minutes, the parents are going to notice the sky changing and start packing up the food quickly, putting the sandwiches and fruit back into the basket before they get ruined. The lemonade jug will probably get sealed up too so it does not spill. They are going to call the children over to stop playing frisbee and help gather everything, and the kids might be a little disappointed. After that, the family will likely fold up the blanket and head toward their car or look for shelter nearby. If the rain starts before they finish packing, they might end up getting a little wet.", "tips": "Use the wind and clouds as your main evidence and build a clear step by step packing up sequence. Mention how the children's activity will be affected too, since a full answer covers everyone in the scene.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_35_a.jpg"}, {"id": 36, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the child is going to keep reaching for that cookie jar and try to grab a cookie, since the lid is already open and the jar is within reach. In the next few seconds, because the stool looks wobbly, there is a real chance the child could lose balance while stretching up on tiptoes. The jar itself might get bumped during the reach, so it could wobble or even tip toward the edge of the counter. If the parent in the doorway happens to look into the kitchen at the right moment, they will probably rush over to steady the stool and catch the child before anything falls. But if they do not notice in time, the child might actually get a cookie, or the stool could tip and the child might need to grab the counter. Either way, I think this is going to end with the parent finally noticing and coming to help.", "tips": "Highlight the wobbly stool as the main source of risk and predict a couple of different possible outcomes using might and could. Bring the background parent into your story as the person likely to intervene.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_36_a.jpg"}, {"id": 37, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think that dog is going to try to get to the chicken very soon since nobody is watching it and the plate is right at the edge of the counter. In the next few seconds, the dog is going to stand up on its back legs, maybe even put its paws on the counter, and try to grab a piece with its mouth. Because the plate is so close to the edge, it might get knocked over or slide off if the dog bumps into it, and there could be a loud crash if it falls to the floor. Since someone seems to be coming down the hallway, a family member is probably going to walk in right at that moment, and they will likely catch the dog in the act or find the mess. After that, they will probably scold the dog gently and clean up the fallen food.", "tips": "Use the dog's body language, paws lifted and nose pointed up, as clear evidence it is about to act. Predict the near miss or accident with the plate, then bring in the approaching family member as a natural resolution.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_37_a.jpg"}, {"id": 38, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the friend with the glove raised is going to catch the baseball in the next second or two, since the ball is already flying straight toward him and he looks focused and ready. After he catches it, I think he is going to throw it right back to his friend so they can keep playing catch for a while longer. Since the toddler on the blanket is watching the ball go up in the air, the child might get excited and point at it or maybe even try to stand up to get a better view. The little dog on the leash is also looking up, so it could bark at the ball or try to chase after it if its owner is not holding the leash tightly. If the dog pulls hard, the owner might have to calm it down or move it a bit further away from where the friends are playing. Overall, I think the game of catch is going to continue smoothly, and the toddler and dog will just keep watching from the side.", "tips": "Since his hands are already raised and his eyes are on the ball, you can predict the catch with strong certainty using is going to. Add the toddler and dog reactions as smaller, less certain predictions using might or could.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_38_a.jpg"}, {"id": 39, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the woman is going to walk over to that plant and water it soon, since she is already looking at it with a concerned expression and the soil clearly looks dry and cracked. In the next minute or so, I think she is going to pick up the watering can from the table and pour some water directly into the pot. After she waters it, I do not think the plant is going to look better right away, since the leaves are already drooping and a bit yellow, but she will probably keep an eye on it over the next few days to see if it recovers. She might also move the pot to a spot with better light or trim off the yellow leaves at the edges to help it grow back healthier. If the plant does not improve after watering, she could decide to research what else it needs or even ask someone for advice. Overall, I think she is going to try her best to save this plant from dying completely.", "tips": "Use the dry cracked soil and her worried expression together to justify a confident prediction that she will water it. Extend your answer by predicting what happens after the watering too, not just the single action.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_39_a.jpg"}, {"id": 40, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Predict what you think will happen next in this scene.", "model_answer": "I think the man is going to change that flat tire himself in the next few minutes, since the trunk is already open and the spare tire and jack are right there ready to use. He is checking his watch, so I think he is worried about being late for something, maybe a meeting, and that is probably going to make him work quickly. I imagine he will start by taking out the jack and lifting the car up before removing the flat tire and putting the spare one on. Since another car is approaching slowly with its hazard lights on, I think that driver might be stopping to offer help, especially if they can see he is stuck on the side of the highway. If they do stop, the two of them could work together to finish the tire change faster than he would alone. Eventually, I think he is going to get back in his car and continue driving, though he will probably still be running a bit behind schedule.", "tips": "The open trunk with tools already visible is a strong clue he will fix it himself, so predict that with confidence. Use the approaching car with hazard lights to add a second, less certain prediction about someone stopping to help.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_40_a.jpg"}, {"id": 41, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your friend is looking for a new apartment and has narrowed it down to these two choices. Look at both pictures and convince your friend which one to choose.", "model_answer": "I think you should choose the downtown apartment. First, since you work downtown, you would save almost an hour every day by not commuting, and that time really adds up over a week. Second, having a gym right in the building means you would actually use it, instead of paying for a membership somewhere else. Yes, the suburban apartment is bigger and has a lovely backyard garden, which would be great if you had a family or wanted more space to relax. But right now, you are single and focused on your career, so convenience matters more than square footage. Being fifteen minutes from a grocery store is not a big deal, you can shop on your way home. Overall, the downtown apartment fits your lifestyle much better, it saves you time, keeps you active, and puts you close to everything you need.", "tips": "Push the comparison further than just listing features, tell your friend WHY convenience matters for their specific life right now. Use time saved and lifestyle fit as your strongest persuasive points.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_41_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_41_b.jpg"}, {"id": 42, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your cousin has received two job offers and wants your opinion. Look at both pictures and persuade your cousin which job to accept.", "model_answer": "I would tell my cousin to take the job at the small accounting firm. Even though the corporate job pays more, think about what you are giving up, two hours a day commuting and a stressful, noisy office. That adds up to real exhaustion over time, and it can hurt your health and your relationships. The small firm offers a short commute and a relaxed atmosphere, which means you actually enjoy going to work every morning. The salary is lower, I understand that is a real downside, but you can always negotiate a raise once you prove yourself. Money is not everything if the job makes you miserable. The corporate path does offer faster promotion, that is tempting for your career, but burning out in the first year would not help you either. For your overall happiness and long term health, the small firm is clearly the better choice.", "tips": "Acknowledge the higher salary directly since it is the obvious pull of the corporate job, then explain why work-life balance outweighs it. Naming the health and relationship cost of a long commute makes the argument concrete.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_42_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_42_b.jpg"}, {"id": 43, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your family is planning a vacation and cannot agree on where to go. Look at both pictures and persuade your family which destination is the better choice.", "model_answer": "I really think we should go with the mountain cabin trip. First of all, it is only a three hour drive, so we save a lot of money and stress compared to an eight hour flight, and we do not have to deal with airports or jet lag. Second, the lake and hiking trails give us plenty to do outdoors, and honestly, spending time in nature together as a family sounds really relaxing. I know the beach resort sounds appealing because everything is included and the weather is warm, but it will be extremely crowded that week, and standing in lines all day is not exactly a vacation. The cabin trip has fewer activities for the little ones, that is true, but we can bring games and there is still fishing and swimming in the lake. Given the shorter travel time and the peaceful setting, the mountain cabin is clearly the smarter choice for our family this year.", "tips": "Lead with the practical advantage, shorter travel time, since it is the easiest point for a family to agree on. Address the crowding at the beach resort directly, it is the strongest counterargument you need to defuse.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_43_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_43_b.jpg"}, {"id": 44, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your coworker wants to try a new restaurant for a team lunch and is deciding between these two. Look at both pictures and persuade your coworker which one to pick.", "model_answer": "For the team lunch, I would pick the sushi restaurant. The biggest reason is the variety, we have a few vegetarians on the team, and the sushi place has plenty of vegetarian and vegan rolls, so nobody gets left out. Also, since we only have an hour for lunch, the fast service really matters, we cannot afford a fifteen minute wait for a table like at the Italian place. I know the Italian restaurant has a cozier atmosphere and bigger portions, which sounds nice for a relaxed meal, but it is not practical for a workday lunch with a tight schedule. The sushi restaurant is a bit noisy near the kitchen, that is a fair point, but we can ask for a table further away. Considering the time limit and the need to include everyone's dietary preferences, the sushi restaurant is honestly the better option for our team today.", "tips": "Ground your persuasion in the team's real constraints, time limit and dietary needs, rather than just personal taste. Concede the noise issue but offer a simple workaround to keep your argument strong.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_44_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_44_b.jpg"}, {"id": 45, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your neighbour is buying a used car and has found these two options. Look at both pictures and persuade your neighbour which car is the better buy.", "model_answer": "I would tell my neighbour to buy the sedan. The low mileage and clean maintenance record mean fewer surprise repair bills down the road, and that gives real peace of mind with a used car. The fuel economy is also much better, which saves real money every month, especially with gas prices these days. I understand the SUV has all wheel drive, and that is a big plus for winter roads here, plus more cargo space is convenient for hauling things. But that SUV also has higher mileage and a cracked windshield that needs fixing right away, so the upfront savings disappear fast once you add repair costs. If winter driving is the main concern, good winter tires on the sedan would handle most conditions just fine. Overall, the sedan is the more reliable and cost effective choice, especially for someone buying a used car on a budget.", "tips": "Turn the winter-driving advantage of the SUV into a manageable concern by suggesting winter tires as an alternative. Emphasize hidden costs like the cracked windshield to strengthen the case for the cheaper-to-maintain option.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_45_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_45_b.jpg"}, {"id": 46, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your sister is choosing a gym membership and is deciding between these two options. Look at both pictures and persuade your sister which gym to join.", "model_answer": "I think you should join the small neighbourhood gym. Since it is only a five minute walk from your place, you are much more likely to actually go regularly, and that consistency matters more than fancy equipment. Also, having a personal trainer included for free is a huge benefit, especially since you have mentioned wanting guidance with your workouts. The big chain gym does have a pool and more variety with group classes, which sounds appealing, but you told me evenings are the only time you can go, and that gym gets extremely crowded then, plus parking is limited. Waiting for machines every night would get frustrating fast. Sure, the neighbourhood gym has more basic equipment, but it covers everything you actually need for strength training. Given how close it is and the free personal training, the small gym is clearly the more practical choice for your routine.", "tips": "Connect the location advantage directly to consistency, a gym someone will actually visit beats one with more features. The free personal trainer is a strong, specific selling point, use it as your second reason.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_46_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_46_b.jpg"}, {"id": 47, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your friend is choosing a school for their child and has visited these two schools. Look at both pictures and persuade your friend which school is the better choice.", "model_answer": "I would recommend the small school within walking distance. The class size is the biggest factor for me, eighteen students means the teacher can actually give your child individual attention, while thirty students in a class makes that much harder. Also, since it is walking distance, your child avoids a thirty minute bus ride every morning and afternoon, which is exhausting for a young kid. The large school does have great sports facilities and modern computer labs, and if your child loves sports, that is worth considering. But the small school's strong arts program could be just as valuable, especially if your child enjoys drawing or music. Yes, the building is older, but that does not affect the quality of teaching or your child's experience day to day. When you weigh the smaller class sizes and the short walk against a longer bus ride, the small school seems like the better fit for your child.", "tips": "Class size is usually the strongest persuasive point for a school comparison, lead with it. Acknowledge the sports program's appeal honestly, then pivot to the arts program as an equally valid alternative interest.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_47_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_47_b.jpg"}, {"id": 48, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your brother is choosing a new phone and cannot decide between these two. Look at both pictures and persuade your brother which phone to buy.", "model_answer": "I think you should go with the budget phone. First, the battery life is a huge advantage, lasting a day and a half means you are not constantly worrying about finding a charger, especially since you travel a lot for work. Second, it costs significantly less, and you told me you wanted to save money this year for your trip. The newest phone does have a better camera and a bigger, brighter screen, which would be nice if photography was your main hobby, but you have said you mostly just take quick photos for messaging, so the camera difference will not matter much day to day. Running out of battery by the afternoon would honestly be more frustrating than a slightly lower resolution photo. Considering your budget goals and how much you rely on your phone lasting all day, the budget phone is clearly the smarter and more practical choice for you.", "tips": "Tie your reasons back to the listener's actual habits, mention travel or budget goals to make the persuasion feel personal, not generic. Directly compare the camera quality tradeoff against the battery life advantage rather than ignoring it.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_48_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_48_b.jpg"}, {"id": 49, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your roommate wants to buy a used sofa for the living room and found these two options online. Look at both pictures and persuade your roommate which sofa to buy.", "model_answer": "I really think we should get the smaller two seat sofa. The most important thing is that it actually fits through our doorway without a problem, while the sectional barely fits and might even get damaged trying to move it in. That alone is a big risk not worth taking. It is also much cheaper, and honestly, we agreed to keep our spending down this month, so that price difference matters. I know the sectional looks more comfortable and would be great for movie nights with a big group, but how often do we actually have that many people over at once? Not very often, if we are being honest. If friends do visit, we can always bring in extra chairs or floor cushions. Given the doorway issue and the price, the two seat sofa is clearly the more sensible choice for our apartment right now.", "tips": "The doorway problem is a concrete, almost undeniable practical objection, use it as your opening argument. Soften the seating limitation by offering a simple real world solution like extra chairs.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_49_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_49_b.jpg"}, {"id": 50, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your spouse is choosing between two part time work schedules offered by their employer. Look at both pictures and persuade your spouse which schedule to choose.", "model_answer": "I would choose the four day schedule with the longer shifts. Yes, ten hour days sound tiring, but think about what you get in return, three full days off every single week. That is so much more time for us to relax together, run errands without rushing, and even take short trips. With the five day schedule, you would be home for dinner each night, which is nice, but you would only have two days off, and weekdays would still be packed with errands and chores. Being tired after a long shift is a real downside, I will not pretend otherwise, but you could rest on your days off and still have plenty of family time overall, just concentrated differently. Missing a few weeknight dinners feels like a fair trade for three full days together every week. Considering how much extra free time it gives us as a family, the four day schedule is clearly the better option.", "tips": "Reframe the tradeoff as concentrated time versus spread out time, this framing makes the persuasion feel balanced rather than one sided. Be honest about the fatigue downside before countering it, this builds credibility.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_50_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_50_b.jpg"}, {"id": 51, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your neighbour plays loud music late at night several times a week, and it keeps you awake before work. You have not spoken to them about it yet, but the noise is affecting your sleep and your job performance. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would knock on my neighbour's door at a reasonable time, maybe on a weekend afternoon, so I could speak calmly instead of complaining late at night. I would say something like, hi, I don't want this to be awkward, but the music has been pretty loud a few nights this week, and it's been keeping me up before work. I'd ask if they could turn it down after around ten or eleven, or maybe use headphones in the evening. I would keep my tone friendly and not accusing, since they might not realize how well sound travels through the walls. I would also offer to let them know if I'm ever too loud, so it feels like a two way conversation. If the noise continued, I would speak to the building manager, but I'd want to give a polite, direct conversation a real chance first.", "tips": "Show that you tried a calm, face-to-face approach before escalating to management. Use polite hedging language like 'I don't want this to be awkward' to sound diplomatic, and mention a specific, reasonable request such as a cutoff time."}, {"id": 52, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "A close friend borrowed your winter coat two months ago and still has not returned it, even though you have mentioned it twice already. You need the coat now that the weather is getting colder. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "Since I've already mentioned it twice and it hasn't worked, I would send my friend a direct but friendly message instead of letting it slide again. I would say something like, hey, I really need my winter coat back this week since it's getting cold, could you drop it off or should I come pick it up? I would suggest a specific day and offer to meet halfway, so there's no excuse for further delay. I would keep my tone light, maybe adding a small joke, so my friend doesn't feel attacked, but I would still be firm that this needs to happen soon. If they still didn't return it, I would probably go to their place myself to get it, because at that point it's clearly not just forgetfulness. I value the friendship, but I also need to be practical about my own needs.", "tips": "Because two earlier hints failed, show escalation by proposing a concrete day and a pickup plan rather than another vague reminder. Balance firmness with warmth so the answer doesn't sound like an accusation of theft."}, {"id": 53, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "At work, a coworker presented an idea to your manager during a meeting that was actually your idea, without giving you any credit. Your manager praised the coworker in front of the whole team. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would first talk to my coworker privately instead of confronting them in front of everyone, because I'd rather solve this professionally than create a scene. I would say something like, hey, I noticed you presented the idea we discussed last week, and it would mean a lot if you credited me next time, since I put a lot of thought into it. I'd stay calm and give them a chance to explain, since it's possible they didn't mean to leave me out. If it happened again, I would follow up with my manager directly and calmly explain my contribution, maybe by showing notes from when I first came up with the idea. I would focus on the facts rather than sounding bitter, and make it clear I just want fair recognition going forward, not conflict. Protecting my professional reputation matters, so I wouldn't just let it go.", "tips": "Model a two-step approach: a private, non-confrontational talk with the coworker first, then escalating to the manager only if it happens again. Mention keeping evidence like notes or emails, which sounds realistic and mature."}, {"id": 54, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your landlord promised three weeks ago to fix a leaking faucet in your kitchen, but nothing has been done, and the leak is getting worse. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would contact my landlord again, but this time in writing rather than just a phone call, so there's a clear record of the request. I would write something like, hi, I'm following up on the kitchen faucet leak I reported three weeks ago, it hasn't been fixed and it's actually gotten worse, so I'd appreciate a repair scheduled this week. I would stay polite but specific about the timeline, since a vague follow-up hasn't worked. I might also mention that the leak could cause water damage if it's left much longer, which affects the property too. If I still didn't get a response within a few days, I would look into my province's tenant rights to understand what steps I could take next, like contacting a housing authority. Being clear, keeping things in writing, and staying professional gives me the best chance of getting this resolved.", "tips": "Emphasize switching from a verbal to a written request for a paper trail, a realistic Canadian tenant strategy. Mention potential property damage and knowing tenant rights as next steps if the landlord still doesn't respond."}, {"id": 55, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your aunt came to visit for what was supposed to be a weekend, but it has now been ten days and she has not mentioned when she plans to leave. You need your space back and have upcoming plans. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "This is tricky because I care about my aunt and don't want to seem rude, but I would sit down with her and bring it up gently over a cup of tea rather than dropping it suddenly. I would say something like, I've really loved having you here, but I have some plans coming up next week that I need to prepare for, so I wanted to check what your plans are for heading back. I'd frame it around my own schedule rather than blaming her for overstaying, which feels less like a rejection. If she seemed unsure, I might gently suggest a date, like maybe this weekend would work well before things get busy for me. I would also say I've enjoyed the visit, so she doesn't feel unwelcome, just that timing needs to shift. Family situations like this need honesty delivered kindly.", "tips": "Because this involves family, stress warmth and appreciation alongside the boundary-setting, not just the request to leave. Frame the ask around your own schedule ('I have plans') rather than blaming the aunt directly."}, {"id": 56, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "A friend is often twenty to thirty minutes late whenever you make plans together, and it has started to feel disrespectful of your time. You have a lunch planned with this friend tomorrow. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would bring this up with my friend before tomorrow's lunch, maybe today over text, rather than waiting and getting frustrated again in the moment. I would say something like, hey, I love hanging out with you, but I've noticed I end up waiting a while whenever we meet, and it makes me feel like my time isn't valued, so I wanted to mention it before tomorrow. I would keep my tone caring rather than angry, since the goal is to fix the pattern, not to guilt them. I might suggest a practical solution too, like agreeing on a slightly later time that works for both of us, so there's less pressure to rush. During lunch, if they arrived late again, I would calmly mention I'd already ordered, just so there's a small natural consequence. Honest, gentle communication works better than silently building resentment.", "tips": "Show initiative by addressing the pattern before the next meetup, not just reacting once more. Suggest a practical compromise like adjusting the agreed time, which demonstrates problem-solving, not just complaining."}, {"id": 57, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You share an apartment with a roommate who consistently avoids doing dishes, taking out the garbage, or cleaning shared spaces, leaving most of the work to you. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would ask my roommate to sit down for a few minutes so we could talk it through calmly, rather than bringing it up angrily while doing the dishes myself again. I would say something like, hey, can we figure out a better system for chores, I've noticed I've been doing most of the cleaning lately and it's starting to feel unfair. I'd suggest a simple, specific schedule, like alternating who takes out the garbage each week and who handles dishes, so expectations are clear instead of vague. I would try to stay solution focused instead of listing every past frustration, since that usually makes people defensive. I might also suggest posting the schedule on the fridge as a visual reminder. If things didn't improve, I would bring it up again more firmly, but most roommate issues improve once there's a clear, fair system both people agreed to.", "tips": "Focus on proposing a concrete system, like a rotating chore schedule, rather than just venting about unfairness. Mention a visual reminder like a posted schedule, a practical detail that makes the answer sound realistic and specific."}, {"id": 58, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You picked up your car from an auto repair shop and were charged significantly more than the original estimate, without anyone explaining the extra charges beforehand. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would ask to speak with the manager or the mechanic directly, staying calm but firm, since getting upset usually doesn't help. I would say something like, I noticed the final bill is quite a bit higher than the estimate I was given, could you walk me through exactly what extra work was done and why. I would ask them to show me the original quote alongside the final invoice so we could compare the numbers together. If the extra charges were for work I never approved, I would explain that I should have been contacted before any additional work was done, and ask them to remove or reduce those charges. I would keep receipts and photograph both documents in case I needed to follow up, maybe even with a consumer protection office if the shop refused to cooperate. Staying polite but persistent gives me the best chance of a fair resolution.", "tips": "Emphasize comparing the original estimate to the final invoice side by side, a concrete detail that shows problem-solving. Mention that unapproved extra work should have required prior contact, a reasonable consumer expectation in Canada."}, {"id": 59, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "A friend keeps giving you unwanted advice about your career choices, even after you have told them you appreciate their concern but want to make your own decisions. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "Since I've already hinted at this before and it hasn't changed anything, I would need to be more direct the next time it comes up, while still being kind about it. I would say something like, I really do appreciate that you care about my career, but I've realized I need to make these decisions on my own, even if I make mistakes, so I'd love it if we could talk about other things instead. I would explain that hearing constant advice actually makes me feel less confident, not more supported, so being honest about that impact might help them understand why it matters to me. I would also redirect the conversation gently in the moment, maybe by changing the subject, so it doesn't turn into another debate. If they brought it up again, I would probably just say, remember what we talked about, and leave it there.", "tips": "Since a previous hint failed, this answer needs to show a clearer, more direct boundary this time, explaining the emotional impact ('makes me feel less confident'). Include a short phrase to use in future moments, showing you have a repeatable strategy."}, {"id": 60, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "A colleague constantly interrupts you during team meetings whenever you try to share an update, which makes it hard for you to finish your points. Explain what you would say to resolve this situation.", "model_answer": "I would probably address this in two ways, first in the moment and then in a private conversation afterward if it kept happening. During the meeting, I would say something simple and polite like, let me just finish this point and then I'd love to hear your thoughts, which sets a boundary without sounding harsh. Afterward, I would find a quiet moment to talk to my colleague privately and say, I've noticed I sometimes get interrupted during meetings, and I wanted to mention it because I want my updates to come across clearly to the team. I would keep my tone collaborative rather than accusing, since they might not realize they're doing it. I might also suggest we both try to let each other finish talking before jumping in, framing it as a shared team habit to improve. Clear, respectful communication, both in the meeting and privately, usually resolves this pattern well.", "tips": "Show a two-part strategy: a polite in-the-moment phrase to reclaim the floor, plus a private follow-up conversation. Frame the fix as a shared team habit rather than a personal attack, which sounds more professional and diplomatic."}, {"id": 61, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people prefer to work from home, while others prefer to work in an office with their colleagues. Which do you think is better for most workers? What is your opinion? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, working from home is better for most workers, and I have a few reasons for thinking this way. First of all, working from home saves a huge amount of time and money. For example, my cousin used to commute an hour each way to her office in Toronto, and now that she works from home, she uses that extra time to exercise and cook healthy meals instead of sitting in traffic. Second, I think people are actually more productive at home because there are fewer interruptions. In an office, someone is always stopping by your desk to chat or calling a meeting that could have just been an email, but at home you can focus on your tasks without constant distractions. Third, working from home gives people more flexibility to balance their family life and their job. A friend of mine has two young kids, and being at home means she can pick them up from school and still finish her work later in the evening. Of course, some people miss the social side of an office and that is a fair point, but overall I believe the benefits of working from home, like saved time, better focus, and more flexibility, outweigh the drawbacks. That is why I think working from home is the better choice for most workers today.", "tips": "State your opinion in your very first sentence so the listener knows your position right away. Use specific, concrete examples like the commute story rather than vague statements, and end by clearly restating your opinion in different words."}, {"id": 62, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people enjoy living in a big city, while others prefer the quiet of a small town. Which do you think offers a better quality of life? What is your opinion? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "I personally believe that a small town offers a better quality of life than a big city, and let me explain why. To begin with, small towns tend to have a much stronger sense of community. Where I grew up, everyone at the local grocery store knew my name, and neighbours would actually check in on each other, which is something you rarely see in a big city like Toronto or Vancouver where people barely know who lives next door. Second, the cost of living is usually much lower in a small town. Housing prices in a small town can be half the price of similar housing in a major city, which means people have more money left over for their families and less financial stress overall. Third, small towns usually have less traffic and pollution, so daily life feels calmer and healthier. My uncle moved from Calgary to a small town in Alberta a few years ago, and he says his commute went from forty minutes to eight minutes, and he feels much less stressed now. I understand that big cities offer more job opportunities and entertainment, and that is definitely true, but for everyday quality of life, I think the peace, affordability, and community feeling of a small town make it the better option. That is why I would choose small town living over city living.", "tips": "Pick a clear side immediately, big city or small town, and do not waver. Use real comparisons like cost of living or commute time to make your reasons concrete instead of abstract."}, {"id": 63, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people prefer to take public transit, while others prefer to drive their own car. In your opinion, which is the better way to get around a city? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, public transit is the better way to get around a city, and I have several reasons for saying this. First, public transit is much better for the environment. When hundreds of people take one bus or subway train instead of driving separate cars, it clearly reduces the amount of pollution and traffic congestion in the city. Second, public transit is usually cheaper than owning and driving a car. When you think about gas, insurance, parking fees, and maintenance, driving a car adds up to a lot of money every month, while a monthly transit pass is much more affordable. For example, a friend of mine sold his car after moving downtown and now saves almost four hundred dollars a month by taking the subway to work instead. Third, taking public transit lets you use your time more productively, because you can read a book, answer emails, or just relax instead of focusing on the road. Of course, I recognize that driving offers more freedom and convenience, especially if you live far from a transit line or need to travel with a lot of bags, and that is a valid point for some people. Still, when I consider the cost savings, the environmental benefits, and the ability to use my commute time wisely, I believe public transit is generally the smarter choice for getting around a city.", "tips": "Since this is a direct comparison, briefly acknowledge the other side once but spend most of your time supporting your own choice. Use numbers, like money saved per month, to make your reasons feel real and specific."}, {"id": 64, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people prefer shopping online, while others prefer shopping in physical stores. Which method of shopping do you think is better? What is your opinion? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "I believe that shopping online is better than shopping in physical stores, and I want to share a few reasons why. First of all, online shopping is incredibly convenient because you can shop anytime, day or night, without leaving your home. For instance, last winter during a huge snowstorm, I was still able to order groceries and household items online and have them delivered right to my door, which would not have been possible if I had to drive to a store. Second, online shopping usually offers a much wider selection of products, and it makes comparing prices between different stores very easy. In just a few minutes, I can check three or four websites and find the best deal, whereas walking around a mall to compare prices would take hours. Third, online reviews from other customers are extremely helpful for making good decisions. Before I buy anything expensive, like a laptop or a piece of furniture, I always read the reviews first, and this has saved me from making several bad purchases in the past. I do understand that some people enjoy physically trying on clothes or seeing a product in person before buying it, and that is a reasonable preference. However, considering the convenience, the wider selection, and the helpful reviews, I still believe that online shopping is the better option overall for most everyday purchases.", "tips": "Keep your three reasons distinct from each other, convenience, selection, and reviews, so you are not repeating the same idea in different words. A short personal story, like the snowstorm example, makes your answer memorable."}, {"id": 65, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some parents give their children smartphones at a young age, while others wait until their children are teenagers. What is your opinion on when children should be given a smartphone? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, parents should wait until their children are at least teenagers before giving them a smartphone. First, young children need to develop real face to face social skills, and too much phone time gets in the way of that. When I watch my neighbour's eight year old play with her friends outside instead of staring at a screen, I can see how much more creative and social she is compared to kids who are always on a device. Second, smartphones expose children to content that is not appropriate for their age, such as violent videos or messages from strangers, and young children do not always have the judgment to handle that safely. Third, giving a phone too early can lead to problems with sleep and focus at school, because children often stay up late scrolling instead of resting properly. My sister delayed giving her son a phone until he turned thirteen, and she noticed his grades and sleep both improved once he learned to manage his time with it. Of course, some parents feel a phone is necessary for safety reasons, like contacting a child after school, and that concern is understandable. Still, I believe the risks to development and online safety outweigh the convenience, so waiting until the teenage years is the wiser choice.", "tips": "This topic invites strong feelings, so state your position firmly in the first sentence and stay consistent throughout. Acknowledging the safety-phone counterargument briefly shows balanced thinking without weakening your main opinion."}, {"id": 66, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe it is important for adults to keep learning new skills throughout their lives, while others believe learning is mainly for young people. What is your opinion? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "I strongly believe that adults should keep learning new skills throughout their entire lives, not just during their school years, and I have several reasons for this opinion. First, learning new skills keeps the mind sharp and can even help prevent memory problems as people age. My grandmother started learning to paint at the age of seventy, and not only does she enjoy it, but her doctor told her that staying mentally active like this is genuinely good for her brain health. Second, the job market is always changing, so adults who keep learning new skills are much more likely to stay employed and adapt to new technology. For example, a coworker of mine took an evening course in computer software last year, and that new skill directly helped her get promoted at work. Third, learning something new simply brings a lot of personal satisfaction and confidence. When I taught myself to cook proper meals a couple of years ago, it gave me a real sense of accomplishment and improved my daily life in a practical way. Some people might argue that learning gets harder as you age or that adults are too busy with work and family, and I understand that challenge is real. However, I still believe the mental, career, and personal benefits make lifelong learning worth the effort at any age, which is why I see it as essential for adults.", "tips": "Use a range of examples across different life stages, like a grandmother and a coworker, to show your opinion applies broadly, not just to one narrow situation. Keep transitions like first, second, and third clear so the structure is easy to follow while speaking quickly."}, {"id": 67, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that everyone should volunteer in their community, while others think volunteering should be a personal choice. What is your opinion on the importance of community volunteering? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, community volunteering is extremely important, and while I would not force anyone to do it, I strongly believe more people should choose to get involved. First, volunteering directly helps people in need who might otherwise be forgotten, such as seniors, newcomers, or families struggling financially. For example, I volunteer once a month at a local food bank, and I have seen firsthand how much a simple box of groceries can mean to a family going through a hard time. Second, volunteering builds a stronger sense of community, because it brings neighbours together and creates connections between people who might never have met otherwise. When our neighbourhood organized a park cleanup day last spring, I ended up meeting several people who live just a few streets away from me. Third, volunteering is genuinely good for the volunteer as well, since it reduces stress and gives people a real sense of purpose. After a busy work week, spending a few hours helping others makes me feel more grounded and grateful for what I have. Some people say they do not have time to volunteer, and I understand that life can be busy, but even a small amount of time given regularly can make a meaningful difference. That is why I see community volunteering as something everyone should try to make room for.", "tips": "Explain benefits both to others and to the volunteer personally, since that gives you two distinct angles to support your opinion. A specific example, like a food bank or park cleanup, is more convincing than a general statement about helping people."}, {"id": 68, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe social media has made friendships weaker, while others believe it has made friendships stronger. What is your opinion on how social media affects friendships? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, social media has generally made friendships weaker rather than stronger, and I would like to explain why I feel this way. First, many people now substitute liking a post or leaving a quick comment for actually calling a friend or meeting up in person, and this can make relationships feel shallow over time. I have noticed that some of my own friendships have become mostly online interactions, and honestly, they do not feel as close as they used to when we saw each other regularly. Second, social media often creates unnecessary comparison and jealousy between friends, because people usually only post the exciting or happy parts of their lives. Seeing a friend's vacation photos or a new house can sometimes cause quiet resentment instead of genuine happiness for them, which slowly damages the friendship. Third, spending too much time scrolling through social media can actually take time away from real conversations. My roommate and I used to talk every evening after dinner, but now we often just sit together scrolling on our phones instead of really talking. On the other hand, I do admit social media helps people stay in touch with friends who move far away, and that is a real benefit. Still, I believe the loss of deep, meaningful interaction outweighs that convenience, which is why I think social media has weakened friendships overall.", "tips": "This is an opinion many people disagree about, so make sure every example you give actually supports weaker friendships rather than describing something neutral. Briefly admitting one benefit of social media, like staying in touch long distance, shows fair judgment."}, {"id": 69, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people prefer to cook meals at home, while others prefer to eat out at restaurants regularly. In your opinion, which habit is better for a person's overall well-being? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, cooking meals at home is much better for a person's overall well-being than eating out at restaurants regularly. First, cooking at home gives you complete control over the ingredients you use, which makes it easier to eat healthier food. When I cook my own dinner, I can choose fresh vegetables and control the amount of salt and oil, but restaurant meals are often loaded with extra sodium and hidden calories that I cannot see or control. Second, cooking at home saves a significant amount of money over time. A restaurant meal can easily cost fifteen or twenty dollars, while a similar meal at home might only cost five, and those savings add up over a month. My sister started meal prepping on Sundays instead of ordering takeout, and she says she now saves almost two hundred dollars a month. Third, cooking can be a relaxing and rewarding activity that reduces stress, especially when you try a new recipe or cook with family members. I find that preparing a meal after a long day helps me unwind more than sitting in a busy restaurant. I understand eating out saves time and offers convenience for busy families, and that is a fair point. Still, considering the health, financial, and personal benefits, I believe cooking at home is the better habit overall.", "tips": "Compare home cooking and eating out across three different areas, health, money, and stress, so your answer feels well-rounded rather than repetitive. A concrete dollar amount, like the restaurant versus home-cooked meal cost, makes your reasoning persuasive."}, {"id": 70, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe apartments are a suitable place to keep pets, while others believe pets should only live in houses with yards. What is your opinion on keeping pets in an apartment? Support your answer with reasons.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, apartments can absolutely be a suitable place to keep pets, as long as the owner is responsible and chooses the right kind of pet. First, many smaller pets, like cats or small dog breeds, do not actually need a large yard to be happy, as long as they get enough attention and exercise indoors. My neighbour keeps a cat in her one bedroom apartment, and the cat seems perfectly content playing with toys and lounging near the window all day. Second, apartment living often means the owner takes their dog outside for walks multiple times a day, which can give the pet more consistent exercise than a dog left alone in a backyard for hours. A friend of mine walks her dog three times a day around the neighbourhood, and that dog probably gets more exercise than some dogs that just sit in a yard. Third, most apartment buildings today have rules and even amenities, like dog parks or pet washing stations, designed to support pet owners, which shows apartment living and pet ownership can work well together. Of course, very large or highly active dog breeds probably do need more outdoor space than a typical apartment can offer, and that is a fair concern. Still, for most common pets, I believe a responsible owner in an apartment can provide a happy and healthy life.", "tips": "Address the likely counterargument, that pets need yards, directly but briefly, then explain why your opinion still holds for most common pets. Specific everyday examples, like a cat by the window or daily walks, make an abstract opinion feel practical and real."}, {"id": 71, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "Okay, this picture shows something pretty strange. There is a small dog sitting behind the wheel of a little red toy car, the kind kids usually pedal around the driveway. The dog is wearing round black sunglasses, which makes it look like it is cruising along like a tiny celebrity. Its front paws are resting right on the plastic steering wheel, as if it actually knows how to drive, which is impossible. Behind the car, a child is running and laughing, probably the owner of the toy, trying to catch up to the dog. Normally you would expect a child to be driving this kind of toy car, not a dog, and you definitely would not expect a dog to be wearing sunglasses like a person. It is such an odd, funny scene because the dog looks so confident, like it planned this whole thing itself.", "tips": "Focus on the contrast between what a toy car is normally used for and the dog taking over that role. Use words like 'strange', 'unexpected', and 'as if' to signal the humour, and describe the sunglasses and steering wheel detail clearly since those are the strongest visual clues.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_71_a.jpg"}, {"id": 72, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a man doing something you would never expect to see at a bus stop. He is dressed in a full business suit and tie, and right there on the sidewalk he has set up a small portable ironing board. He is actually ironing a shirt while he waits, and the iron seems to be plugged into a cord running to a nearby lamppost. Around him, other commuters are standing normally, waiting for the bus, and a few of them are clearly staring at him because this is not something you would expect to see in public. Ironing is usually something people do at home, in private, not on a busy street corner. It looks like he might be running late, but the whole scene is unusual because of where he chose to do this task.", "tips": "Emphasize the mismatch between a private household chore and a public location. Describe the other commuters' reactions to show why it is surprising, and mention the practical detail of the cord and lamppost to sound specific and observant.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_72_a.jpg"}, {"id": 73, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "In this picture, there is an orange tabby cat sitting right in the middle of a kitchen sink, and the strange part is that the sink is completely full of dirty dishes. There are soapy plates and cups all around the cat, but it looks perfectly calm, almost like this is its favourite spot in the house. Standing nearby at the counter is a woman with her hands on her hips, and her expression shows she is quite surprised, probably because she was about to wash those dishes and did not expect to find her cat in there. Normally a sink is used only for washing dishes, not as a resting spot for a pet, so seeing the cat curled up among the plates and cups is really unexpected. It is funny because the cat seems completely unbothered, while the woman clearly cannot believe what she is seeing.", "tips": "Highlight the cat's calm body language versus the woman's surprised reaction, since that contrast drives the humour. Mention specific sink contents like plates and soap bubbles to add concrete visual detail to your answer.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_73_a.jpg"}, {"id": 74, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This is a really unusual scene because a man is riding a unicycle, which already takes a lot of balance, but at the same time he is walking five dogs. He is holding all five leashes in one hand while steering with the other, and the dogs are different breeds and sizes, so some run quickly while others trot more slowly beside him. He is wearing a helmet, which suggests he takes this seriously and wants to stay safe. In the background, people sitting on benches in the park have turned to watch him because this is not something you see every day. Normally people walk dogs on foot, and riding a unicycle is already a rare skill by itself, so combining both at once makes the picture look almost like a circus act in an ordinary park.", "tips": "Break the description into the two combined challenges, riding a unicycle and controlling five dogs, so the listener understands exactly why it is impressive and odd. Mention the onlookers' reactions to round out your sixty seconds naturally.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_74_a.jpg"}, {"id": 75, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a young man doing something quite strange in a park. He is standing completely upside down on his head, balanced against a tree trunk, and somehow he is still calmly reading a paperback book held above him. He has headphones on too, so he seems totally relaxed, almost as if standing on his head is the most normal thing in the world for him. In the background, a couple of joggers are running along the path, going about their normal exercise routine, which makes his upside-down reading look even stranger by comparison. Normally people read books while sitting or lying down, not while balancing on their head against a tree, so this combination of reading and doing a headstand at once is really unexpected and a bit funny.", "tips": "Point out the contradiction between an active headstand and a passive activity like reading, since that combination is the core of the humour. Describe the joggers in the background briefly to show the normal park setting around this odd moment.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_75_a.jpg"}, {"id": 76, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows something you would never expect to find in a parking lot. Someone has set up a complete living room in the middle of the empty pavement, with a sofa, a coffee table, a floor lamp, and even a small area rug underneath it all. A man is sitting comfortably on the sofa, and directly in front of him there is a television on its own stand, and he seems to be watching it just like at home. Around this living room, the rest of the lot is completely empty, with painted lines on the ground and no cars nearby, which makes the furniture look out of place. Normally a living room belongs inside a house, not out in a parking lot. It is a surprising sight, cozy and ordinary, except it is sitting outdoors in a spot meant for parking cars.", "tips": "List the furniture pieces in order, sofa, table, lamp, and rug, to show you noticed the full setup rather than just one item. Contrast the coziness of the room with the emptiness of the parking lot to make the oddity clear.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_76_a.jpg"}, {"id": 77, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a huge inflatable dinosaur that somehow ended up stuck in an office hallway. The dinosaur is several metres tall and wedged in sideways, completely blocking the narrow hallway from one wall to the other. On each side of it, two employees dressed in normal business clothes are trying to figure out how they are going to move this giant thing past the doorways without damaging anything. Office hallways are normally used for walking between meeting rooms, not for storing giant inflatable toys, so seeing something this large stuck in such a tight space is really unexpected. It looks like the dinosaur was meant for some kind of event, but whoever brought it in did not think about whether it would fit through the hallway. The whole situation looks a bit chaotic.", "tips": "Describe the scale problem clearly, a huge dinosaur versus a narrow hallway, since that mismatch is the whole joke of the picture. Mention the employees' confused reaction to add a human element to your description.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_77_a.jpg"}, {"id": 78, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a chef doing something quite unusual for the weather around him. He is wearing his full white chef's uniform and tall chef's hat, but over top of that he has also put on a heavy winter coat, which already looks strange together. He is standing outside in a backyard completely covered in snow, and snow is still falling around him, yet he is grilling food on a barbecue as if it were a warm summer day. Smoke rises up from the grill, standing out against the white snow behind him. Normally people use a barbecue in warm weather, not in the middle of a snowy winter scene. It is surprising to see him so focused on cooking despite the cold, almost like he refuses to let the weather stop him.", "tips": "Focus on the seasonal contradiction, barbecuing is usually a summer activity, so doing it in the snow is the key unusual detail. Mention both his chef uniform and winter coat together, since wearing both at once adds another layer of oddity.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_78_a.jpg"}, {"id": 79, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a man walking down a city sidewalk dressed in a really mismatched way. From the waist up, he is wearing a thick winter coat, a scarf wrapped around his neck, and mittens on his hands, which makes him look dressed for a freezing cold day. But from the waist down, he is wearing plain summer shorts and sandals, the complete opposite of what his top half suggests. Around him, other pedestrians are dressed normally for warm weather, in light shirts and short sleeves, and a few glance at him curiously because his outfit does not make sense together. Normally people choose clothing that matches the season from head to toe, not a strange mix of both at once. It looks like he got dressed in a rush and grabbed completely different outfits for his top and bottom.", "tips": "Clearly separate the description into top half and bottom half so the listener understands exactly how mismatched the outfit is. Use the other pedestrians' normal clothing as a comparison point to highlight just how out of place his outfit looks.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_79_a.jpg"}, {"id": 80, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Look at the picture below. Describe this unusual situation in as much detail as you can.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a man doing something that makes no logical sense, but he seems completely serious about it. He is standing on a city street wearing rubber boots, and in front of him there is only a small rain puddle on the pavement, nothing more. Despite this, he is holding a proper fishing rod and casting his line right into the puddle, treating it exactly like a real lake or river. Nearby on the sidewalk, a woman walking her dog has stopped to watch him, and her expression shows she is quite puzzled. Normally people go fishing at lakes, rivers, or the ocean, somewhere with enough water for fish to live, not in a tiny puddle left by rain. It is such an unexpected sight because he follows every normal step of fishing where catching anything is impossible.", "tips": "Emphasize the impossibility of fishing in a puddle to explain why the scene is funny, then describe his serious, focused behaviour as if it were completely normal for extra contrast. Include the puzzled bystander to show how others in the scene react to him.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_80_a.jpg"}];
